bodyfat.csv -- percent body fat and thirteen body measurements for 252 men
==========================================================================

Vendored 2026-08-28 for reproduce/experiments/ch5_end_to_end.py (checklist C3).
20,001 bytes, 253 lines (header + 252 records, no trailing newline), 15 columns.
sha256 4da0e256cb3ebd9c4e5591c32131d31a5e81eb5e7d353555c19bb4addded9a55

Committed BYTE-FOR-BYTE as received, including the two leading-dot tokens noted
below and the missing final newline. Do not reformat it: a full-matrix diff
against the canonical file gives 0 differing cells, and that is what pins both
the provenance and the identity of the BodyFat column. A tidy-up would cost the
only evidence that this is the dataset it claims to be.


SOURCE
------
StatLib Datasets Archive, "bodyfat", Carnegie Mellon University -- the 252x15
form whose column order is exactly this header.
    http://lib.stat.cmu.edu/datasets/bodyfat
StatLib now returns 403 to non-browser clients. The scholarly archive carries
the same data as a 252x19 superset, of which this file is the 15-column
projection:
    https://jse.amstat.org/datasets/fat.dat.txt

CITATION
--------
Johnson, R. W. (1996). "Fitting Percentage of Body Fat to Simple Body
Measurements." Journal of Statistics Education, 4(1).
doi:10.1080/10691898.1996.11910505

Measurements supplied by Dr. A. Garth Fisher, Human Performance Research Center,
Brigham Young University, and first reported in:
    Penrose, K. W., Nelson, A. G., & Fisher, A. G. (1985). Medicine and Science
    in Sports and Exercise, 17(2), 189 (abstract).
    doi:10.1249/00005768-198504000-00037

No page numbers for Johnson: JSE volume 4 was electronic-only and Crossref
returns no page field. Do not invent one. Penrose and Nelson are initials-only
in the publisher record -- only Fisher's given name is attested. Do not expand
them. UCI does not host this dataset; do not cite it or Kaggle as the source.


TERMS -- READ BEFORE REDISTRIBUTING
-----------------------------------
This file is NOT under the repository's GPL-3.0 licence, and must not be
treated as though it were. GPL-3.0 grants commercial use; the data owner did
not. There is no CC, ODbL, OSI licence or public-domain dedication anywhere.
What exists is a personal permission grant, verbatim from
https://jse.amstat.org/datasets/fat.txt:

    "The data were generously supplied by Dr. A. Garth Fisher, Human
    Performance Research Center, Brigham Young University, Provo, Utah 84602,
    who gave permission to freely distribute the data and use them for
    non-commercial purposes."

Redistribution inside this non-commercial dissertation harness is expressly
covered. Commercial use is not granted.

Frequently misquoted as the data licence, and is not: the JSE *article* page
carries "Copyright (c) 1996 by Roger W. Johnson, all rights reserved..." That
governs Johnson's prose, not these numbers.


COLUMNS -- MIXED UNIT SYSTEM, WHICH IS THE USUAL TRAP
-----------------------------------------------------
    Density   body density from underwater weighing, gm/cm^3
    BodyFat   percent, SIRI's estimate (not Brozek) -- see LEAK below
    Age       years
    Weight    POUNDS
    Height    INCHES
    Neck, Chest, Abdomen, Hip, Thigh, Knee, Ankle, Biceps, Forearm, Wrist
              circumferences, CENTIMETRES

Body size is imperial, every circumference is metric. Abdomen is StatLib's
"Abdomen 2", at the umbilicus and level with the iliac crest; Biceps is
extended; Wrist is distal to the styloid processes.


THE LEAK: Density IS the target, in another coordinate
------------------------------------------------------
Siri's two-compartment equation (lean density 1.10, fat density 0.90):

    percent body fat = 495 / Density - 450

BodyFat was COMPUTED from Density by that formula. Any model given both is
being handed its own answer. Measured on this file:

    as shipped                                  R^2 = 0.9773
    with Johnson's three density fixes applied  R^2 = 0.9990
    excluding cases 48, 76, 96, 182, 169        R^2 = 1.0000

The relation is deterministic, not merely strong -- the residual is entirely
errata. `reproduce/experiments/ch5_end_to_end.py` therefore drops Density in
every arm, and any future use must do the same.

(Brozek's alternative, which this file does NOT use, is 457/D - 414.2.)


ERRATA -- ALL PRESENT AND UNCORRECTED HERE
------------------------------------------
Johnson's documented list, from the JSE fat.txt SPECIAL NOTES. He writes "among
others", so it is explicitly non-exhaustive.

    case  42  Height 29.50 in at 205 lb -> should be 69.50. The only row with
              Height < 40; implied BMI 165.6. Not re-derivable from this file,
              because the adiposity column that proves it is not in the
              15-column projection.
    case  48  Density 1.0665 -> 1.0865   (fix the density, not the percent)
    case  76  Density 1.0666 -> 1.0566
    case  96  Density 1.0991 -> 1.0591   worst row, 17.0 pp discrepancy
    case 182  BodyFat recorded as .0 -- a floor truncation of Siri = -3.61%,
              not a measurement. Censored, not zero.

Found by arithmetic against the 19-column superset during the 2026-08-28
provenance pass, and NOT in any published errata list:

    case 169  the Siri column is wrong: 34.3 recorded, D = 1.0180 implies
              36.25, and the Brozek column confirms the density is right. This
              corrupts the regression TARGET by ~2 pp.
    case 200  same pattern, smaller: 23.6 recorded against 23.14 implied.
    case   6  both percentages sit below what D = 1.0502 implies, so the
              density is the suspect column -- but no single-digit change fixes
              it. Flag, do not fix.
    case 221  Weight 153.25 lb contradicts both dropped columns; 173.25
              reproduces adiposity and fat-free weight simultaneously.
    case 163  unresolved -- listed adiposity contradicts Weight/Height, and no
              quarter-inch height reconciles it. Flag, do not fix.

NOT errata, despite looking wrong: case 216 (Density .9950, below water, 47.5%)
and case 39 (363.15 lb) are extreme but internally consistent across all four
derived columns in the superset.

DO NOT "clean" this file by recomputing BodyFat from Density. It would silently
alter roughly 33 of 252 rows, including every case the literature analyses.

PARSER NOTE: cases 182 and 216 store leading-dot tokens (`.0`, `.9950`). A
strict numeric parser must accept them. pandas.read_csv does.
